    "notes": [
      "Map_a, map_b, headline and session_bias_check are DESCRIPTIVE and IN-SAMPLE: thresholds are chosen and evaluated on the same scores, and the EER is an interpolated crossing between observed thresholds, not a realisable threshold. They compare representations under one protocol; they are not calibrated error rates for unseen identities.",
      "This protocol is KNOWN-POPULATION CROSS-IDENTITY verification, not open-set identification and not a FaceID/deployment estimate: every impostor is one of the same 40 Olivetti identities, so no unknown identity enters the descriptive block.",
      "Score = minimum Euclidean distance from a probe image to the 5 enrolment images; decision = accept if score <= threshold. Trials are full-dimensional, no PCA.",
      "Impostor trials: for each identity, the 390 images (all 10 images of each of the other 39 identities) are scored against that identity's enrolment.",
      "Trial granularity: 200 genuine trials per split give FRR resolution 0.5%; 15,600 impostor trials give FAR resolution 0.0064%. Map B's mean EER (0.22%) is below the genuine-trial grid resolution, so it is 'at or below the measurement floor'.",
      "calibration_heldout_evaluation is the separate honest check: thresholds are chosen on 13 calibration identities and applied to 13 identity-disjoint evaluation identities, with 14 identities enrolled in no gallery used as unknown-to-gallery probes. All 40 identities still come from Olivetti; the 5 partitions overlap and are not independent samples. It is a small-sample optimism check, not a deployment estimate.",
      "Calibration FAR targets are 1% (primary) and 0.1% (secondary). No FRR target is used because the 65-genuine calibration fold has 1/65 = 1.54% FRR granularity, so a 1% FRR target is unresolvable and would force a degenerate infinite threshold.",
      "The threshold_curves figure is drawn from the descriptive genuine/impostor scores pooled over the 5 primary splits."
    ]
  },
